security_aggregate:
  overall: LOW
  auth_modes_relevant:
  - LOGIN_FORM
  - OAUTH2
  - LDAP
  ingestion_filter_relevance: false
  authorization_consistency:
    verdict: UNGATED
    detail: 'All 5 AlertController endpoints carry no @PreAuthorize and no SECURITY_RULES
      entry — fall through to .authenticated() only. getAllAlerts method-level sidecar
      verified absence: `SecurityConstants.java:98-295` contains `PUT /api/alerts/{alert_id}/status`
      rule but NO entry for `GET /api/alerts`. Per-entity alert listings on DataEntityController
      also lack permission gates. Only `updateAlertConfig` is gated (`DATA_ENTITY_ALERT_CONFIG_UPDATE`
      per SecurityConstants.java:304-307). The registered `changeAlertStatus` rule
      pairs `ALERT` resource type with `DATASET_FIELD_ADD_TERM` permission constant
      (SecurityConstants.java:295-296) — a cross-domain enum mismatch is itself a
      finding (getAllAlerts.md:security.known_security_gaps.[1] severity MEDIUM).
      **Batch H confirms at SQL primary source**: ReactiveAlertRepositoryImpl has
      NO authorization annotations whatsoever on 19 methods; defence-in-depth is null;
      a direct service-bypass invocation reaches the SQL with no caller-ID check.
      However, `getDataEntityIdByAlertId` PARTICIPATES in authorization indirectly
      — AlertResourceExtractor calls it to resolve the DataEntity ID for SecurityConstants.SECURITY_RULES
      lines 295-296 permission evaluation on PUT /api/alerts/{alert_id}/status.'
  owner_scoping:
    verdict: MIXED
    detail: '`getAllAlerts` returns all alerts platform-wide via flat `WHERE STATUS
      = OPEN` jOOQ query with no owner join (ReactiveAlertRepositoryImpl.java:142-157)
      — admin-path-without-admin-gate. `getAssociatedUserAlerts` (`/my`) and `getDependentEntitiesAlerts`
      (`/dependents`) are owner-scoped at runtime via reactor Context principal resolution
      — correctness delegated to AlertService.listByOwner / listDependentObjectsAlerts.
      `changeAlertStatus` does NOT check the caller owns the alert or its referenced
      data entity (BYPASSES at the mutation path). Live alerting doc recommends getAllAlerts
      tab for ''stewards and admins watching the full alert surface'' — doc-code audience
      mismatch (getAllAlerts.md:docs_link_semantic.doc_drift_findings.[0]). **Batch
      H confirms**: per-method at SQL primary source — BYPASSES: listAllWithStatusOpen,
      getAlertsByDataEntityId, getAlertsCountByDataEntityId, countAlertsWithStatusOpen,
      countDependentObjectsAlerts; RESPECTS (via caller-supplied ownerId; service
      trusts the caller): listByOwner, countAlertsWithStatusOpenByOwner, getObjectsOddrnsByOwner,
      listDependentObjectsAlerts. The trust boundary is the service layer, not the
      repository.'
  strengths:
  - 'Service-layer reopen-guard: `AlertServiceImpl.updateStatus` rejects manual reopen
    with 400 if open alert of same type already exists for the entity (AlertServiceImpl.java:124-131)'
  - Closed enum AlertStatus (OPEN / RESOLVED / RESOLVED_AUTOMATICALLY) — clients cannot
    introduce new states without OpenAPI change
  - '@ActivityLog AOP records ALERT_STATUS_UPDATED on every service-mediated mutation
    (AlertServiceImpl.java:112-113)'
  - '**Batch H — FOR UPDATE on the ingestion-side `getOpenAlertsForEntities` is deliberate
    and well-commented**: lines 130-132 explicit comment frames the FDQT (Failed-Data-Quality-Test
    alerts from multiple sources) concurrent-write race intent; the lock-on-read on
    ingestion path is intentional defence-in-depth at the SQL layer. The mutation-path
    asymmetry (no FOR UPDATE on UI-driven updateAlertStatus) is a separate decision
    — the platform tolerates the read-then-write race on user-driven status changes.'
  - '**Batch H — `USER_OWNER_MAPPING.DELETED_AT IS NULL` LEFT JOIN soft-delete-aware
    convention**: applied consistently across 3 read sites (lines 84-85, 106-107,
    499-502) for resolving alert.status_updated_by username string → OwnerPojo. A
    soft-deleted user-owner binding silently breaks the join (alert returned, OwnerPojo
    resolves NULL on LEFT JOIN) — the audit identity is durable, the OWNER display
    is best-effort.'
  weaknesses:
  - '`getAllAlerts` returns the entire platform''s alert stream to ANY authenticated
    user — no admin gate, no role check (severity HIGH per AlertController + getAllAlerts.md:security.known_security_gaps.[0];
    rediscovered REFACTOR-024 per orchestrator note). **Batch H — SQL PRIMARY-SOURCE
    CONFIRMED**: ReactiveAlertRepositoryImpl.java:143-145 is the canonical SQL — `selectFrom(ALERT).where(ALERT.STATUS.eq(OPEN))`
    with no owner / role / permission filter. The outer-select pagination wrapper
    (createAlertJoinQuery lines 474-483, createAlertOuterSelect lines 485-507) adds
    DATA_ENTITY + USER_OWNER_MAPPING + OWNER + ALERT_CHUNK joins but NO owner predicate.
    8th-sidecar surface in the read-collaborative cross-owner invariant.'
  - '`changeAlertStatus` accepts mutation with no permission gate — any authenticated
    user can resolve/reopen any alert by id'
  - Reopen-conflict guard is read-then-write without serialisable fence — two concurrent
    OPEN requests for sibling alerts on same entity can both pass the guard (severity
    MEDIUM). **Batch H — SQL PRIMARY-SOURCE CONFIRMED**: openAlertWithTheSameTypeExistsForDataEntity
    (lines 397-420) is read-only SELECT EXISTS over a self-join CTE; updateAlertStatus
    (lines 297-306) is UPDATE with no FOR UPDATE. No DB-level UNIQUE (data_entity_oddrn,
    type) WHERE status = OPEN partial-index constraint to back-stop the race.
  - Owner-scoped `/my` / `/dependents` rely on reactor Context propagation — a misordered
    WebFilter could silently degrade to empty or unscoped output
  - 'Spec declares NO security: block on alert-tagged operations and NO error-response
    contracts (only `200`); consumers reading the spec cannot derive auth requirements'
  - Under auth.type=DISABLED, anonymous read of every alert AND anonymous mutation
    of any alert
  - Pagination unbounded at all three layers (controller, AlertApi interface, repository)
    — `size=1_000_000` produces unbounded jOOQ query (getAllAlerts.md:bugs_limitations_corner_cases.[1]
    severity MEDIUM)
  - 'Asymmetry between sibling endpoints: `PUT /api/alerts/{alert_id}/status` IS in
    SECURITY_RULES; `GET /api/alerts` is absent — no defending comment or annotation
    (getAllAlerts.md:security.known_security_gaps.[1])'
  - '**NEW BATCH H — AlertManager webhook bypasses S2S/IngestionDataEntitiesFilter
    (HIGH)**: `AlertManagerController.alertManagerWebhook` (`AlertManagerController.java:21-26`)
    is at `POST /ingestion/alert/alertmanager` — a path the ingestion filter does
    NOT match (the filter matches `/ingestion/entities` POST only). Combined with
    `auth.type=DISABLED`, any caller can POST AlertManager-shaped payloads and create
    OPEN alerts attributed to ANY data entity ODDRN — the payload''s `labels.entity_oddrn`
    field is untrusted. `AlertServiceImpl.java:178` sets dataEntityOddrn from external
    label without validation against the calling principal. (ReactiveAlertRepositoryImpl.md:security.known_security_gaps.[1])'
  - '**NEW BATCH H — `createAlerts` has NO ON CONFLICT semantics (MEDIUM)**: lines
    332-342 build INSERT ... RETURNING with no `.onConflict(...)` clause and no SELECT-then-INSERT
    idempotency check. The AlertManager webhook path (`handleExternalAlerts`) calls
    this WITHOUT the `AlertActionResolver` de-duplication used by the ingestion path
    (`applyAlertActions`) — a Prometheus retry on a transient network error produces
    duplicate ALERT rows.'
  cross_file_inconsistencies:
  - AlertController per-class mutations are ungated; DataEntityController.updateAlertConfig
    (the per-entity halt-config mutation) IS gated by DATA_ENTITY_ALERT_CONFIG_UPDATE
    — same conceptual feature, two different gating models
  - 'Spec-tag:alert reports `security: []` at spec; AlertController reports auth-mode-only
    at runtime — same conclusion (no permission gate) but reached via different layers'
  - Live alerting doc names the All-tab audience as 'stewards and admins'; code enforces
    'any authenticated user' — doc-code audience divergence (getAllAlerts.md:docs_link_semantic.doc_drift_findings.[0])
  - '**Batch H — AlertHousekeepingJob operates on this repository''s tables but uses
    a SEPARATE JDBC `Connection` (not R2DBC)** — AlertHousekeepingJob.java:24 (sync)
    vs JooqReactiveOperations.java:28 (reactive). The two share no transaction scope.
    A read in this repository can interleave with a housekeeping DELETE producing
    either state (row present or Mono.empty); no FOR SHARE lock on read paths to prevent
    this. The race is benign (eventual consistency) but the documented manual-RESOLVED
    bug at AlertHousekeepingJob.java:28-34 means reads silently shrink as housekeeping
    purges past TTL.'
