performance_aggregate:
  overall: MEDIUM
  hot_paths:
  - GET /api/alerts (`getAllAlerts`) — paged list across global alert population,
    primary 'All' view, fired on every alerts UI tab activation alongside /api/alerts/totals;
    downstream is paged SELECT FROM alert WHERE status=OPEN with no owner join via
    createAlertJoinQuery (getAllAlerts.md:performance.hot_paths.[0])
  - GET /api/alerts/totals (`getAlertTotals`) — three count queries on every alerts-page
    render to populate tab badges — AlertController
  - PUT /api/alerts/{alert_id}/status (`changeAlertStatus`) — single-item mutation,
    plus @ActivityLog AOP fires synchronous activity-row INSERT every call — AlertController.changeAlertStatus
  - POST /ingestion/alert/alertmanager — webhook from Prometheus AlertManager, can
    fire multiple-times-per-minute on noisy systems (group_interval default 5m) —
    AlertManagerController (separate concept; cross-references)
  - '**Batch H — `listAllWithStatusOpen` SQL primary-source detail**: window-fn-based
    pagination (`COUNT(*) OVER () + ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ...)`) materialises
    the total count for the entire result set BEFORE returning the page slice; for
    a platform with 100K+ open alerts this is full ALERT scan + page slice in one
    query (JooqQueryHelper.java:73-90).'
  - '**Batch H — `getOpenAlertsForEntities` is on the ingestion hot path** (every
    ingestion request via AlertServiceImpl.applyAlertActions → AlertActionResolver).
    The FOR UPDATE clause holds row-level locks on every matched ALERT row for the
    duration of the surrounding @ReactiveTransactional — on high-ingestion-rate platforms
    with many concurrent DQ tests, this is a write-bottleneck on the ALERT table.'
  - '**Batch H — `createAlerts` runs on every ingestion request producing new alerts**.
    Batched at 1000 per partition via executeInPartitionReturning; typical ingestion
    produces O(1)-O(100) alerts per request (single batch); a backwards-incompatible-schema-change
    across 5 datasets each with 100 fields would still fit in one batch.'
  throughput_shape:
    verdict: SINGLE_ITEM_ONLY
    detail: 'No batch resolve / batch reopen / batch list endpoint. A consumer needing
      to resolve 100 alerts must issue 100 sequential PUT calls. Pagination is offset-based
      only (no cursor) on the three list endpoints. **Batch H confirms batched ingestion-side
      writes via 1000-row partitions but no public bulk-mutation API.**'
  scaling_shape:
    verdict: UNPAGINATED
    detail: 'Pagination params (page/size) declared but unconstrained at controller
      (no @Max). Spec carries no `maximum` on `SizeParam`; spec-conformant `size=2147483647`
      is permissible. No streaming, no SSE. OFFSET=(page-1)*size means deep-pagination
      cost grows linearly with page; keyset pagination not implemented (getAllAlerts.md:performance.known_performance_gaps.[1]).
      **Batch H — `(page-1)*size` produces negative offset for page=0** at lines 147,
      169, 189, 233 (no @Min(1) clamp); Postgres returns 500 error.'
  strengths:
  - All 5 endpoints reactive `Mono<ResponseEntity<...>>` — non-blocking I/O (AlertController.java:21,
    30, 36, 44, 52)
  - Stateless controller — horizontal scaling unconstrained at the controller layer
  - AlertController constructor-injected as singleton (no per-request bean creation
    overhead)
  - '**Batch H — single-round-trip reads via jsonArrayAgg for ALERT_CHUNK materialisation
    (line 80)** — no per-chunk fan-out; one query per AlertDto. Batch INSERT via
    executeInPartitionReturning at 1000-row partitions keeps ingestion-side writes
    bounded to ⌈N/1000⌉ round-trips.'
  weaknesses:
  - No upper bound on `size` parameter — caller can request arbitrary response body
    and DB load
  - No caching on `getAlertTotals` — every alerts-page render re-runs the three count
    queries; for high-traffic platforms this is per-page-load DB hit
  - No request-throughput observability (no `@Timed`, no Micrometer counter, no structured
    logging on entry/exit)
  - Reopen-conflict guard is not transactionally fenced (read-then-write race window)
  - Single-item-only `changeAlertStatus` — bulk operator workflows pay N round-trips
  - Activity-row INSERT fires on every status change regardless of whether status
    actually changed (no idempotency / no-op short-circuit)
  - Deep-pagination on getAllAlerts uses OFFSET arithmetic — Postgres scans and discards
    (page-1)*size rows on every deep-page request (getAllAlerts.md:performance.known_performance_gaps.[1])
  - No HTTP-level test exists for `GET /api/alerts` — regressions in OpenAPI routing,
    WebFlux config, Jackson, or jOOQ silently break the endpoint with the build green
    (getAllAlerts.md:bugs_limitations_corner_cases.[3])
  - '**NEW BATCH H — `listByOwner` empty-result total uses platform-wide count (MEDIUM
    UX bug)**: line 177 passes `countAlertsWithStatusOpen()` (counts ALL platform-wide
    open alerts) into pageifyResult as the empty-record total counter, instead of
    `countAlertsWithStatusOpenByOwner(ownerId)`. When the owner has zero alerts, the
    UI''s pagination badge reports the platform-wide count, not the owner-scoped count
    (which would be 0). Latent: surfaces only on empty result; visible only as a UI
    badge / pagination metadata mismatch. (ReactiveAlertRepositoryImpl.md:bugs_limitations_corner_cases.[5]
    + performance.known_performance_gaps.[0])'
  - '**NEW BATCH H — Recursive lineage CTE in `listDependentObjectsAlerts` has no
    upper bound (LOW)**: cycle-detection works via `parent_oddrn || parent_oddrn`
    array-membership check but no recursion-depth ceiling, no LIMIT, no Postgres `WITH
    RECURSIVE ... CYCLE` clause. A runaway recursive CTE on a heavily-fanout graph
    manifests as Postgres CPU saturation, not a clean application error.'
  - '**NEW BATCH H — `jsonArrayAgg` for ALERT_CHUNK materialisation may produce very
    large JSON per alert (LOW)**: for an alert with thousands of chunks (chronically-failing
    DQ test ingested over a year), the JSON aggregate inflates the row size; cost
    of serialising/deserialising 10MB JSON per row is non-trivial.'
  - '**NEW BATCH H — No method-level observability** (no `@Timed`, no Micrometer counters,
    no structured log entries on any of 19 methods). Latency regressions invisible
    at the repository boundary.'
  cross_file_inconsistencies:
  - Cursor pagination on getDataEntityActivity (DataEntityController) but offset pagination
    on every alert list endpoint — cross-controller inconsistency for what could be
    the same alert-stream concept
  - '**Batch H — `AlertHousekeepingJob` uses synchronous JDBC; this repository uses
    reactive R2DBC** — two share no transaction scope; read-and-housekeeping interleave
    is benign but documented. The manual-RESOLVED housekeeping bug at AlertHousekeepingJob.java:28-34
    is consumer-observable at this repository''s reads (alerts silently disappear
    on next 15-min cycle).'
